WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.000
I really liked your video about North Korea,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.420
and it reminded me of a book I read called "Escape from Camp 14",

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:06.800
that I wanna talk about, but I'm having some trouble finding it,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.560
so I don't know if i'm going to be able to.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.320
Let's just look.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.140
You know, my exceptionally well cataloged home library just isn't as well cataloged as it used to be.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.360
Yes, I found it, Hank!

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.700
Right where it belongs, under the German editions of "let it snow".

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.980
Right, so this is the story of Shin Dong-hyuk, the only person ever known to have escaped Camp 14.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:24.740
Camp 14 is, at least we think, the worst camp for North Korean political prisoners.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.720
Like, the life expectancy for males there is somewhere around 40 if you don't get executed.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.740
And the stories that have emerged from camp 14 are truly horrifying,

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.080
like, for instance, a six-year-old girl who is beaten to death for stealing five kernels of corn.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.280
But what you expect in these stories is that our hero will suffer, and he does.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.300
When Shin is 14, he is imprisoned in solitary confinment,

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.960
where he can't stand up for eight monts and tortured every day.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.840
Also, because he was born in the camp, the child of a marriage arranged by guards,

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.940
he has never in his life eaten a legal meal other than gruel made of cabbage and corn.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:59.780
And because malnutrition is universal in camp 14 and death by starvation is very common,

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.520
access to food is a very important incentive.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.220
By the way, people make jokes about North Korea, but it is not funny.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:06.780
This is not going to be a funny video

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.020
Now, Hank, the other thing that you'd expect in a story like this is that the hero will have an indomitable will,

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.240
and some kind of moral center that carries him through all of his trials and tribulations.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.580
Which why it is so confounding when in the first chapter you learn that this guy, Shin, when he was 14,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.580
turned his mother into the authorities when he overheard her talking about escaping.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.780
And then eventually both Shin's mom and his brother were executed.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.800
Shin witnessed this execution, and at the time, he felt it was appropriate.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.780
Shin was born into a world where if you hear people talking about escaping,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.380
you should tell on them and then they should be executed.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.500
That was moral.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:44.840
Also, he knew from having witnessed it, that if he didn't tell on his family, and they did escape, he would be executed.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.460
Which makes his own escape even more ethically complicated

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:50.320
because he knows he put what remains of his family at risk.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:55.820
And at this you hear Shin tells that his escape wasn't dominated by his indomitable human will seeking freedom.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:56.700
He just wanted food.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.440
He became friends with one of the few people who lived outside of the camp,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.220
and that guy was like: "you should see the food they have in China, it's endless!"

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:07.020
So Shin decided to escape because he wanted the endless food they had in China.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:10.520
Hank, I'll tell you why I've never believed this whole "the human spirit can't be broken" thing.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.280
You may remember that in march of 2007 I was hospitalized with an infection behind my eye.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.880
This infection was not life-threatening Hank, it wasn't even really eye-threatening.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.560
But it was pretty miserable and uncomfortable.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:24.700
And I remember staring at the acoustic ceiling tiles of the hospital at like 2:30 in the morning one time and thinking,

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.020
Ahh, I'm ready for death.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.460
Like, the problem with extracting information via torture, other than it being wrong,

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.640
is not that people won't talk, it's that they will talk so much; they will say anything to make the pain stop.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:37.860
So, the information you get isn't reliable.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.480
What makes this story amazing is not the strength of the human will, but the fragility of it.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:45.740
And what makes Shin a hero is not that he's born with some ethical center, it's that he develops it,

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.980
in China, and South Korea, and the United States, after his escape.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.900
He is, as he says, evolving into a human being.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.040
Most of us undergo that evolution so early that it feels innate.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.700
But North Korea reminds us, it's not.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.160
Hank, I'll see you on friday.

